# Line endings are decided here, not by each clone's `core.autocrlf`.
#
# Every text file is LF in the index and LF in the working tree, on every platform. `text=auto`
# leaves git's own binary detection in charge of what counts as text; `eol=lf` is what makes the
# *checkout* LF too, which is the half `core.autocrlf` was overriding — prettier reads the working
# tree, so without it `format:check` fails locally while CI, which checks out LF, passes.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Vendored third-party code, checked in byte-for-byte.
#
# `-text` disables conversion in both directions, so these keep the exact bytes of the published
# original and stay verifiable — `apps/web/src/components/map/rtl.ts` records the sha256 this file
# is checked against, and that digest is the only review a vendored blob gets.
#
# Must stay *below* the `*` rule: the last matching pattern wins, so moving it above would let the
# global rule renormalise these and break the digest. `-eol` is redundant next to `-text`, which
# already disables conversion, but it keeps `git check-attr` from reporting an inherited `eol: lf`
# on a path that must never be converted.
apps/web/public/vendor/** -text -eol

# Cached Overpass answers for the enrichment benchmark. `text=auto` above would leave the decision
# to git's binary heuristic, which is right about gzip but is a heuristic; a fixture silently
# renormalised is a corrupt archive that fails at `gunzipSync` with nothing pointing here.
packages/ingest/test/fixtures/enrich/*.gz -text -eol
